  "about": {
    "costOfLivingHeading": "Cost of Living",
    "costOfLivingIntro": "This world uses one shared coinage: gold pieces 🟡, silver pieces ⚪, and copper pieces 🟤. Prices, treasury balances, and personal wealth are stored as silver-piece values and displayed as coinage. Grain trades at roughly one silver piece per wain at market (farmgate prices run about 20% lower), anchoring the currency's baseline value. The figures below are storytelling guidelines, not exact simulated totals; actual prices vary with local market conditions.",
    "costOfLivingPeasant": "One peasant (rural resident), bare subsistence for a year: roughly 4–5 copper pieces at the default exchange rates — just enough grain to survive, based on the world's per-capita food need.",
    "costOfLivingUrban": "One town or city resident, for a year: roughly 5–10 copper pieces at the default exchange rates — the same baseline food need, plus higher retail prices and incidental goods city life requires.",
    "costOfLivingFamily": "One family of four or five, for a year: roughly 2–4 silver pieces — the sum of each member's living cost.",
    "costOfLivingHouse": "A modest house or homestead: roughly 10–30 silver pieces — several years of a laborer's savings. Buying property isn't a simulated transaction yet, so treat this as a storytelling rule of thumb rather than an in-game price."
